1
00:00:00,311 --> 00:00:20,500
[SPEAKER_02]: Any such view, well, to the extent that you're still emphasizing the sense-objects, one way or the other, sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, that you emphasize them in terms of you think, at least to some extent, suffering comes from them, is caused by them, to that extent, you lack yoniso manasikāra.

2
00:00:21,841 --> 00:00:26,463
[SPEAKER_02]: So then, if your whole view revolves around pure management, cover-up, denial,

3
00:00:27,944 --> 00:00:31,567
[SPEAKER_02]: of sense objects, because, obviously, they bother you, seemingly.

4
00:00:32,187 --> 00:00:48,739
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't know the escape from them, so you just try to deny the reality of them, through these kind of, as I said, retroactive reinterpretations, and it's like, oh, see, that time when I suffered because somebody said something, well, it was just sound entering my ear, thus suffering was not real.

5
00:00:49,439 --> 00:00:53,482
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you relieve yourself from the burden of carrying it, because you don't know the escape from it.

6
00:00:54,007 --> 00:00:57,210
[SPEAKER_02]: And you don't really relieve yourself, you just cover it up, until it comes back.

7
00:00:57,850 --> 00:00:59,372
[SPEAKER_02]: Until the new sounds touch your ear.

8
00:01:00,172 --> 00:01:10,101
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you try so hard, that when somebody's saying something upsetting to you, to interpret that as sounds touching your ear, but your feelings, they don't work that way.

9
00:01:10,121 --> 00:01:16,746
[SPEAKER_02]: So you're gonna still be upset, even if the next millisecond you manage to plug it, the dukkha still comes first.

10
00:01:17,867 --> 00:01:22,371
[SPEAKER_02]: And the whole thing, the only reason you need to do all of that is because deep down you know that

11
00:01:23,223 --> 00:01:26,185
[SPEAKER_02]: You cannot not be touched by dukkha in the first place.

12
00:01:26,306 --> 00:01:29,688
[SPEAKER_02]: You can only try and manage it and sort of contain it.

13
00:01:30,909 --> 00:01:47,423
[SPEAKER_02]: But the idea of, no, this thing that bothers me, can I reach the place where I'm not bothered to begin with, even if I don't do anything, even if I don't make any effort to look at it this way or that way or focus on this and concentrate on that.

14
00:01:48,243 --> 00:01:50,105
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you realize, oh, that's where dhamma begins.

15
00:01:51,406 --> 00:01:52,527
[SPEAKER_03]: The complete uprooting.

16
00:01:57,527 --> 00:02:02,589
[SPEAKER_02]: And in order to... like somebody who has been... and some... everybody would have been managing the suffering to some extent.

17
00:02:04,410 --> 00:02:09,092
[SPEAKER_02]: If you want to start practicing the practice of uprooting, first thing you need to give up is management of suffering.

18
00:02:09,292 --> 00:02:10,212
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

19
00:02:10,272 --> 00:02:12,653
[SPEAKER_02]: Or seeing at least you're dependent on that measurement.

20
00:02:12,953 --> 00:02:13,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

21
00:02:13,573 --> 00:02:18,035
[SPEAKER_02]: And then... and then work up... work your way towards being able to basically give it up.

22
00:02:18,155 --> 00:02:21,256
[SPEAKER_02]: Because for as long as you don't give it up, you're not starting the practice.

23
00:02:21,276 --> 00:02:21,556
[SPEAKER_01]: You're bound.

24
00:02:21,576 --> 00:02:22,617
[SPEAKER_01]: You're enslaved by it.

25
00:02:23,097 --> 00:02:23,257
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

26
00:02:25,239 --> 00:02:27,561
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, the practice is just kept at the arm's length.

27
00:02:29,603 --> 00:02:32,545
[SPEAKER_02]: Because each time we get into management... You're fueling it.

28
00:02:32,986 --> 00:02:38,770
[SPEAKER_02]: You fuel the implicit, the tacit assumption that suffering did come from that thing that I'm managing.

29
00:02:40,091 --> 00:02:41,393
[SPEAKER_02]: Not from my attitude towards it.

30
00:02:53,928 --> 00:02:57,790
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why yoniso manasikāra is not just like one of the random things that anybody can just do.

31
00:02:58,670 --> 00:03:00,131
[SPEAKER_02]: Because people might develop that notion.

32
00:03:00,791 --> 00:03:03,312
[SPEAKER_02]: Aha, yoniso manasikāra is one of the factors for the right view.

33
00:03:03,332 --> 00:03:05,033
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, so, how do I do yoniso?

34
00:03:05,853 --> 00:03:08,174
[SPEAKER_02]: And then I find the right instruction, and then I get the right view.

35
00:03:08,754 --> 00:03:12,476
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you do yoniso by giving up the management, even before.

36
00:03:15,335 --> 00:03:19,018
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so you see a snake and there's like this fear.

37
00:03:19,558 --> 00:03:40,433
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's like the first thing is to like not try and get rid of that fear, just acknowledge... Well, yeah, like what we were speaking yesterday, like the common notion is you see a snake, there is fear, then you either try to find more information about snake, so to not be afraid, or deny it was a snake to begin with.

38
00:03:40,753 --> 00:03:42,394
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, look, it was actually just a rope.

39
00:03:43,823 --> 00:03:46,685
[SPEAKER_02]: And then the fear disappears and you think you're free now.

40
00:03:47,826 --> 00:04:01,697
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you have even like a slight degree of transparency and honesty, yes, the fear might have subsided now, because you learned the snake is not venomous, or you came closer and you saw that snake was a rope, actually.

41
00:04:02,558 --> 00:04:06,841
[SPEAKER_02]: But then you realize, yeah, okay, but does that mean that I will never in the future experience

42
00:04:07,785 --> 00:04:13,053
[SPEAKER_02]: Anything else that will make me afraid first, even if I can manage it afterwards.

43
00:04:13,533 --> 00:04:21,625
[SPEAKER_02]: And you realize that that is already a problem, because what if you come closer and it's actually even worse snake than you thought it was, and it's not a rope?

44
00:04:23,740 --> 00:04:27,502
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, you will be twice as affected with your fear that already affected you.

45
00:04:27,962 --> 00:04:29,643
[SPEAKER_01]: Or just anything that can evoke fear.

46
00:04:29,924 --> 00:04:30,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

47
00:04:30,344 --> 00:04:31,124
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that there is fear.

48
00:04:31,304 --> 00:04:31,685
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

49
00:04:31,745 --> 00:04:40,550
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the fact that you're liable to it, even if you could prove it wrong afterwards, doesn't make it less real when it affects you.

50
00:04:43,091 --> 00:04:47,514
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the dhamma is not there to disapprove... disprove reality of things.

51
00:04:49,078 --> 00:04:55,400
[SPEAKER_02]: As a way of pasting this view on everything that bothers you, the dhamma is there to teach you how not to be bothered in the first place.

52
00:04:55,440 --> 00:04:59,942
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, you don't care if it's a real snake, or a rope, or a venomous or non-venomous one.

53
00:05:00,082 --> 00:05:01,742
[SPEAKER_02]: You won't be affected by fear either way.

54
00:05:03,403 --> 00:05:04,443
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the true freedom.

55
00:05:06,304 --> 00:05:08,004
[SPEAKER_02]: Not just knowing how to cover it up.

56
00:05:16,867 --> 00:05:18,828
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's like if somebody were to start

57
00:05:24,202 --> 00:05:34,492
[SPEAKER_02]: Sense restraint, keeping the precepts rightly, not... not paired with a view of, oh, if I just do these things mechanically, I'll become a Sotāpanna.

58
00:05:35,152 --> 00:05:45,262
[SPEAKER_02]: But more like, as we discussed for last few days here, when you say no to your desires, when the precepts restrain you, the mind complains, you don't cover it up.

59
00:05:46,309 --> 00:05:50,690
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't give in, you don't psychologize, and you certainly don't try to get rid of it.

60
00:05:51,310 --> 00:05:54,331
[SPEAKER_01]: But you already will be covering up.

61
00:05:54,411 --> 00:05:56,131
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more like seeing that you're covering up.

62
00:05:56,471 --> 00:05:57,131
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

63
00:05:57,192 --> 00:06:04,973
[SPEAKER_02]: So, basically, when I say you're not covering it up, your starting point is covering it up.

64
00:06:05,373 --> 00:06:11,715
[SPEAKER_02]: But now you know that you're not supposed to be covering it up, so you begin to cover it up less, and less, and less, until it's completely uncovered.

65
00:06:12,475 --> 00:06:32,431
[SPEAKER_02]: But, what I'm saying is that, even at that stage, doing that, is closer to yoniso manasikāra, it's closer to that right way of attending things, for what they are, where they are, than doing the yoniso manasikāra on account of you learn what yoniso manasikāra is, and now you're trying to attend things in this different, indirect way, peripherally, and so on.

66
00:06:34,868 --> 00:06:38,869
[SPEAKER_02]: So, through not allowing yourself a cover-up, you are doing the practice.

67
00:06:39,189 --> 00:06:45,131
[SPEAKER_02]: You're doing the yoniso manasikāra, which means it cannot be done the way you conceive doing to be.

68
00:06:47,911 --> 00:06:51,672
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why the more you do it, the more effortless it becomes.

69
00:06:51,853 --> 00:06:53,933
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, actually, there was never effort required.

70
00:06:54,073 --> 00:06:56,274
[SPEAKER_02]: Effort was required in covering it up.

71
00:06:56,294 --> 00:07:01,095
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, effort might be required in abstaining from covering it up, until it's sufficiently uncovered.

72
00:07:07,225 --> 00:07:12,450
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you realize, like, sensuality, following desire, factually takes effort and takes time.

73
00:07:15,414 --> 00:07:19,858
[SPEAKER_02]: Restraining from it is immediately visible and takes no effort and takes no time.

74
00:07:22,020 --> 00:07:25,704
[SPEAKER_02]: Saying no to desire that you didn't follow, that's it.

75
00:07:26,525 --> 00:07:27,146
[SPEAKER_02]: It ended there.

76
00:07:29,405 --> 00:07:30,326
[SPEAKER_02]: You're, you're restrained.

77
00:07:30,506 --> 00:07:35,549
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you don't have to, like, do it, keep doing it or start engaging in the actions in order to do it.

78
00:07:36,089 --> 00:07:43,754
[SPEAKER_02]: You just need to abstain from going down the effort of sensuality that would take time and immediately stay with what was already there.

79
00:07:45,015 --> 00:07:50,378
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, right now, there's already sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, thoughts that don't involve craving.

80
00:07:50,618 --> 00:07:51,559
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no, it's not like that.

81
00:07:51,659 --> 00:07:53,440
[SPEAKER_01]: I need to try and... No.

82
00:07:54,386 --> 00:07:57,815
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, but what person needs to do is start to see them correctly.

83
00:07:57,955 --> 00:08:02,567
[SPEAKER_02]: In other words, recognize sense objects.

84
00:08:03,003 --> 00:08:06,206
[SPEAKER_02]: Sight, sound, smell, taste, touch is that they're not infected with craving.

85
00:08:06,766 --> 00:08:09,188
[SPEAKER_02]: Existing on their own, within the domains of the senses.

86
00:08:10,149 --> 00:08:13,752
[SPEAKER_02]: And then recognize those that are rooted in craving, and they're not giving to those.

87
00:08:14,132 --> 00:08:15,573
[SPEAKER_02]: But you don't have to deny every sight.

88
00:08:16,014 --> 00:08:22,719
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if that were the case, then the Buddha would have agreed with that man who said, how do you practice, you know, taming of your eye?

89
00:08:22,779 --> 00:08:24,881
[SPEAKER_02]: He says, by not seeing forms with my eyes.

90
00:08:25,141 --> 00:08:26,663
[SPEAKER_02]: And he says, well, that's like a blind man.

91
00:08:28,990 --> 00:08:31,711
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not all of the sites that are the problem.

92
00:08:31,771 --> 00:08:39,594
[SPEAKER_02]: If you think all of the sites are the problem, means you don't see the difference between sites affected with craving and sites not affected with craving, which means you don't see the middle way.

93
00:08:41,274 --> 00:08:47,117
[SPEAKER_02]: And for as long as that's not discerned, there is no practice for that person.

94
00:08:49,597 --> 00:08:56,680
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same even with virtue, in terms of the... what you're saying about it's taking no effort and taking no time.

95
00:08:57,573 --> 00:08:58,113
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

96
00:08:58,213 --> 00:09:03,135
[SPEAKER_00]: You can see that same principle on the level of, like, it's only like an act.

97
00:09:03,175 --> 00:09:19,602
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to think of it as an active practice that you need to do for as long as your mode of existence, I suppose, is still, like, dependent on very specific actions that most of the time, even most people are not doing.

98
00:09:20,002 --> 00:09:20,222
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

99
00:09:20,883 --> 00:09:24,764
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize, okay, you just need to kind of stay on this, on this level.

100
00:09:24,824 --> 00:09:26,645
[SPEAKER_00]: It's already kind of there.

101
00:09:27,918 --> 00:09:28,658
[SPEAKER_02]: Without any...

102
00:09:28,698 --> 00:09:30,679
[SPEAKER_02]: Without any... fuel required.

103
00:09:30,719 --> 00:09:31,159
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, exactly.

104
00:09:31,319 --> 00:09:32,119
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the whole point.

105
00:09:37,781 --> 00:09:46,304
[SPEAKER_03]: But isn't it the case that this recognition of those pressures are more like, more gradual?

106
00:09:46,564 --> 00:09:57,248
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you're unable to, right now, right here, just release all the passing pressures, but it's like one step ahead.

107
00:10:01,165 --> 00:10:02,387
[SPEAKER_03]: No, definitely.

108
00:10:02,407 --> 00:10:03,949
[SPEAKER_03]: The whole thing is gradual.

109
00:10:12,964 --> 00:10:15,485
[SPEAKER_02]: You cannot do it just all instantly or all together.

110
00:10:16,186 --> 00:10:21,130
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why it has to start with physical restraint and restraining your senses.

111
00:10:21,210 --> 00:10:28,436
[SPEAKER_02]: So, even if you don't see any of this, it... that's why it has... well, because you don't see any of this, that's the starting point.

112
00:10:28,456 --> 00:10:35,201
[SPEAKER_02]: The person needs to say no to unsuitable sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, even if they don't see the reason why.

113
00:10:37,167 --> 00:10:40,630
[SPEAKER_02]: They don't see a direct connection between saying no to those things and practicing the dhamma.

114
00:10:41,031 --> 00:10:45,455
[SPEAKER_02]: They have to at least start saying no first in order to see the connection with the practice of the dhamma.

115
00:10:46,295 --> 00:10:52,361
[SPEAKER_02]: And that connection would gradually, well, become revealed, be revealed.

116
00:10:52,561 --> 00:10:54,643
[SPEAKER_03]: Like anchored the mind properly.

117
00:10:55,464 --> 00:10:55,744
[SPEAKER_02]: Sorry?

118
00:10:56,024 --> 00:10:57,826
[SPEAKER_03]: Anchored the mind properly.

119
00:10:58,186 --> 00:11:00,088
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that would be the result of it.

120
00:11:00,749 --> 00:11:01,830
[SPEAKER_02]: It wouldn't be your doing.

121
00:11:03,211 --> 00:11:07,235
[SPEAKER_02]: You would anchor the mind by not basically exposing it to the waves.

122
00:11:08,616 --> 00:11:09,417
[SPEAKER_02]: Then it would settle.

123
00:11:10,659 --> 00:11:19,127
[SPEAKER_02]: But you can't just put the anchor down and now it's anchored, while you're still fully allowing it to go among the high waves.

124
00:11:29,949 --> 00:11:46,537
[SPEAKER_03]: Also, not forgetting that this anchor, you, so to speak, you already... your mind is already developed to some degree, but sometimes you can, I don't know, forget or too much go into the senses domain.

125
00:11:46,957 --> 00:11:51,779
[SPEAKER_03]: Is it possible to achieve some mind development?

126
00:11:51,920 --> 00:11:55,061
[SPEAKER_02]: See, forgetting about it, it's not a problem in itself.

127
00:11:57,162 --> 00:11:59,143
[SPEAKER_02]: But acting against it,

128
00:12:01,896 --> 00:12:07,360
[SPEAKER_02]: Through giving in the acts of desire, ill-will, distraction, that's what makes the problem.

129
00:12:08,620 --> 00:12:10,842
[SPEAKER_02]: And those things you cannot... you don't do accidentally.

130
00:12:11,342 --> 00:12:14,044
[SPEAKER_02]: They cannot just happen or you just forgot and you slipped into them.

131
00:12:14,644 --> 00:12:16,325
[SPEAKER_02]: They're always on the level of your choice.

132
00:12:18,707 --> 00:12:28,253
[SPEAKER_02]: Because... and it's important to... to distinguish this because if people don't see that that's where the threshold is, then they'll be afraid of naturally just mind-forgetting stuff.

133
00:12:28,273 --> 00:12:30,715
[SPEAKER_02]: That's just... and that's impossible to stop.

134
00:12:31,785 --> 00:12:32,565
[SPEAKER_02]: Things come and go.

135
00:12:32,625 --> 00:12:34,606
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't possibly know everything all the time.

136
00:12:34,666 --> 00:12:35,526
[SPEAKER_02]: Even the Buddha said it.

137
00:12:35,986 --> 00:12:44,809
[SPEAKER_02]: Nobody, even the supreme, the Tathāgata, is not omniscient, that he knows everything at every second, all the time.

138
00:12:44,829 --> 00:12:45,789
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not how things work.

139
00:12:46,470 --> 00:12:47,310
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's not the problem.

140
00:12:47,370 --> 00:12:56,053
[SPEAKER_02]: But if people don't know that the problem is choices of greed, aversion, delusion, and they have not discerned them sufficiently enough, then they'll fear everything and nothing.

141
00:12:56,133 --> 00:12:57,233
[SPEAKER_02]: So then, when they forget,

142
00:12:59,402 --> 00:13:06,589
[SPEAKER_02]: about anchoring of the mind or the greater context, they break the precepts, because there's nothing in between that they learn how to see.

143
00:13:09,573 --> 00:13:17,561
[SPEAKER_02]: But even then, even when you're the utmost forgetful one, when it comes to breaking the precepts or doing unwholesome things, that still has to be a conscious choice.

144
00:13:18,832 --> 00:13:31,682
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you still go against the precepts and you still go through that choice to break, it means you haven't really understood the weight of that action.

145
00:13:32,522 --> 00:13:36,345
[SPEAKER_02]: You haven't understood the danger of doing so for yourself.

146
00:13:38,087 --> 00:13:41,729
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why one breaks precepts, not because they're forgetful.

147
00:13:44,111 --> 00:13:46,193
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if something becomes very important to you,

148
00:13:47,449 --> 00:13:55,979
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't matter if it's a millisecond or two hours, the importance of it remains, and you can't forget it.

149
00:13:56,039 --> 00:14:03,207
[SPEAKER_02]: As I was giving that simile with a... drinking a beautiful beverage, that then you find out there is poison in it.

150
00:14:05,073 --> 00:14:07,155
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, the beverage is so good, you're addicted to it.

151
00:14:07,355 --> 00:14:08,136
[SPEAKER_02]: Let's put it that way.

152
00:14:08,696 --> 00:14:10,017
[SPEAKER_02]: You've been drinking it your whole life.

153
00:14:10,738 --> 00:14:13,460
[SPEAKER_02]: And now, somebody says there is a poison in it.

154
00:14:13,660 --> 00:14:17,343
[SPEAKER_02]: And you kind of can see for yourself that there probably is a poison in it.

155
00:14:20,086 --> 00:14:27,772
[SPEAKER_02]: The only time you would drink the beverage is if you completely forget that there is a poison in it, which means you haven't really understood the danger of the poison.

156
00:14:29,233 --> 00:14:33,697
[SPEAKER_02]: Or sometimes you might not know how harmful the poison is.

157
00:14:34,966 --> 00:14:42,315
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you haven't investigated enough, and you know there is a poison, but you think you'll be fine, you know, every now and then.

158
00:14:44,838 --> 00:14:46,681
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why you will choose to drink it again.

159
00:14:50,566 --> 00:14:52,888
[SPEAKER_02]: But if, on the other hand, you realized

160
00:14:54,108 --> 00:14:57,029
[SPEAKER_02]: To the extent necessary, the danger of the poison...

161
00:14:57,729 --> 00:15:00,749
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't matter if somebody wakes you in the middle... wakes you up in the middle of the night.

162
00:15:01,929 --> 00:15:06,650
[SPEAKER_02]: If you don't remember anything else, drink means poison, poison means death.

163
00:15:09,031 --> 00:15:13,052
[SPEAKER_02]: And you wouldn't need to go through the motions to remind yourself how not to choose to drink it.

164
00:15:13,612 --> 00:15:18,193
[SPEAKER_02]: You just need to remind... you just need to basically get... see that danger that you would have developed by then.

165
00:15:18,733 --> 00:15:19,933
[SPEAKER_02]: And you wouldn't be drinking it.

166
00:15:20,585 --> 00:15:25,112
[SPEAKER_02]: The simile the Buddha gave is like the the man, um, the leper,

167
00:15:26,113 --> 00:15:35,075
[SPEAKER_02]: He was used to burning his skin with with coal and embers, because he was offering him a relief, but then he finds another medicine, his skin is cured, no more leprosy.

168
00:15:35,875 --> 00:15:38,135
[SPEAKER_02]: Would he still burn himself with those painful coals?

169
00:15:38,235 --> 00:15:38,375
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

170
00:15:38,755 --> 00:15:50,998
[SPEAKER_02]: It's as even if two strong men were to take him under his arm and and drag him into the into the pit of embers, he would want to be everywhere else, anywhere else, except there.

171
00:15:51,824 --> 00:16:05,633
[SPEAKER_02]: He wouldn't need to, like... he wouldn't be able to accidentally choose the embers again, because they are factually unpleasant.

172
00:16:05,853 --> 00:16:12,638
[SPEAKER_02]: Any other perception, other than the perception... Other than the perception of the addicted poison that you've been drinking.

173
00:16:16,881 --> 00:16:19,463
[SPEAKER_02]: But, as I said, you can't mechanically

174
00:16:20,377 --> 00:16:27,379
[SPEAKER_02]: Just think these impersonal abstract thoughts and then magically the choice of choosing the drink disappears.

175
00:16:27,799 --> 00:16:29,500
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's not where the choice was coming from.

176
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:35,242
[SPEAKER_02]: The choice was coming from not recognizing fully that it will kill you if you drink it.

177
00:16:35,902 --> 00:16:36,762
[SPEAKER_02]: As simple as that.

178
00:16:38,322 --> 00:16:43,704
[SPEAKER_02]: So then the practice is to contemplate the danger that it is implicit until it really sinks in.

179
00:16:44,364 --> 00:16:46,305
[SPEAKER_02]: That it will kill you if you drink it.

180
00:16:47,275 --> 00:16:48,136
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, you can't forget it.

181
00:16:48,716 --> 00:16:50,657
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if you forget everything else.

182
00:16:50,717 --> 00:16:53,539
[SPEAKER_02]: You cannot possibly intentionally engage with it ever again.

183
00:16:58,602 --> 00:17:02,505
[SPEAKER_03]: For example, a little bit in different direction.

184
00:17:04,526 --> 00:17:14,493
[SPEAKER_03]: Let's say that one already sees the senses and the sentences, but sometimes they pressure more, and sometimes they lack a more call.

185
00:17:14,953 --> 00:17:16,434
[SPEAKER_03]: And there is this

186
00:17:17,859 --> 00:17:26,023
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, knowledge that this pressure from the senses, it's more intense.

187
00:17:26,603 --> 00:17:26,863
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

188
00:17:26,883 --> 00:17:34,226
[SPEAKER_03]: This is the time when one breaking the precepts, so to speak, because it's... Well, yeah.

189
00:17:34,246 --> 00:17:34,967
[SPEAKER_03]: More intense pressure.

190
00:17:35,567 --> 00:17:35,747
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

191
00:17:35,907 --> 00:17:40,329
[SPEAKER_02]: See, the pressure can be as intense as it wants.

192
00:17:40,809 --> 00:17:42,530
[SPEAKER_02]: It cannot make you act on its own.

193
00:17:45,919 --> 00:17:47,860
[SPEAKER_02]: Only your choice to act makes you act.

194
00:17:48,880 --> 00:17:49,680
[SPEAKER_02]: That's always on you.

195
00:17:52,021 --> 00:17:53,782
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can have the pressure building up, building up.

196
00:17:54,582 --> 00:17:58,063
[SPEAKER_02]: But you are the one who then chooses, I can't take this anymore.

197
00:17:58,363 --> 00:18:00,064
[SPEAKER_02]: Let me do this what I'm pressured towards.

198
00:18:00,724 --> 00:18:06,526
[SPEAKER_02]: In other words, the compulsion to drink this poisonous beverage is so much, I don't care about the poison.

199
00:18:06,846 --> 00:18:08,446
[SPEAKER_02]: But it always has to be your choice.

200
00:18:09,327 --> 00:18:13,548
[SPEAKER_02]: Cannot be just pressure automatically comes and then the action automatically follows.

201
00:18:14,884 --> 00:18:17,847
[SPEAKER_02]: Pressure goes in one direction, action goes in another direction.

202
00:18:18,628 --> 00:18:20,430
[SPEAKER_02]: You are the binding agent.

203
00:18:21,611 --> 00:18:30,200
[SPEAKER_02]: You are the one who does not want the pressure and that's why chooses to go the pathway of following the pleasure, to cover it up, to replace it with.

204
00:18:31,401 --> 00:18:35,405
[SPEAKER_03]: The intensity of this pressure is, from the point of view of

205
00:18:37,257 --> 00:18:40,719
[SPEAKER_03]: Of course, yeah.

206
00:18:40,899 --> 00:18:41,459
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

207
00:18:41,479 --> 00:18:45,141
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you've been practicing restraint and keeping a precept.

208
00:18:45,181 --> 00:18:46,842
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you are not fueling.

209
00:18:48,423 --> 00:18:59,608
[SPEAKER_02]: But, if you want to uproot it once and for all, you have to recognize... Because, by the way, the pressure is now de-intensified, because you haven't been living in the world like before.

210
00:19:01,533 --> 00:19:06,157
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the circumstances have diminished your exposure to the pressure in things.

211
00:19:07,138 --> 00:19:14,304
[SPEAKER_02]: But, if you were to, say, be forced to return and work and live like before, would your mind still remain unbothered?

212
00:19:15,805 --> 00:19:18,828
[SPEAKER_02]: Would you not... would... would... would temptations not exist for it?

213
00:19:21,010 --> 00:19:23,852
[SPEAKER_03]: The life is already diminished, so to speak, probably.

214
00:19:24,673 --> 00:19:28,296
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, it is diminished, but it's diminished because of the circumstances.

215
00:19:31,787 --> 00:19:40,209
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, the fact that... See, you might disagree, but the fact is, you're still doubtful about it, which means you don't know for sure.

216
00:19:41,469 --> 00:19:50,572
[SPEAKER_02]: You like to think it's diminished, and, yeah, there is no problem in thinking it's diminished, but the problem is to think that it is diminished because of something that it's not there.

217
00:19:52,312 --> 00:19:55,213
[SPEAKER_02]: It can only diminish when the danger becomes fully apparent.

218
00:19:55,975 --> 00:19:58,535
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, it doesn't matter the circumstances.

219
00:19:58,595 --> 00:20:01,076
[SPEAKER_02]: Of course, you would still prefer to live a quiet life.

220
00:20:01,656 --> 00:20:08,177
[SPEAKER_02]: But if that changes, war comes, you get put in a prison or whatever, the value of not drinking the poison cannot diminish.

221
00:20:11,018 --> 00:20:17,679
[SPEAKER_02]: And for that value to be fully developed, you need to recognize that no amount of pressure makes you act.

222
00:20:18,799 --> 00:20:24,700
[SPEAKER_02]: You make yourself act because you don't want the pressure, because you want to resist it, because you want it to go sooner.

223
00:20:26,593 --> 00:20:32,015
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why often those... those similes in the suttas of Arahant being like the immovable mountain.

224
00:20:33,116 --> 00:20:39,838
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not that Arahant doesn't experience the... that the mountain doesn't experience the wind, the rain, the snow, the elements.

225
00:20:40,198 --> 00:20:41,419
[SPEAKER_02]: It does, but it's immovable.

226
00:20:42,299 --> 00:20:43,700
[SPEAKER_02]: That cannot make it act.

227
00:20:44,504 --> 00:20:45,384
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the arahant.

228
00:20:46,145 --> 00:20:50,567
[SPEAKER_02]: No amount of stuff can make him do things out of greed, aversion, delusion.

229
00:20:50,667 --> 00:20:51,267
[SPEAKER_02]: Impossible.

230
00:20:51,987 --> 00:20:53,488
[SPEAKER_02]: The connection has been severed.

231
00:20:54,288 --> 00:20:56,649
[SPEAKER_02]: The association has been destroyed.

232
00:20:58,710 --> 00:21:02,472
[SPEAKER_02]: So, when arahant is experiencing pressures of elements or harassment,

233
00:21:04,177 --> 00:21:11,518
[SPEAKER_02]: There is no even an option for his mind to like, oh, let me go back to this desire to to give in, to relieve myself.

234
00:21:11,578 --> 00:21:15,579
[SPEAKER_02]: Because he fully understood that there is no relief in the way he used to think it was.

235
00:21:15,699 --> 00:21:18,579
[SPEAKER_02]: Like burning embers.

236
00:21:18,599 --> 00:21:21,060
[SPEAKER_02]: They don't factually offer relief from leprosy.

237
00:21:21,460 --> 00:21:23,480
[SPEAKER_02]: They just numb you down, because they burn you.

238
00:21:27,461 --> 00:21:33,442
[SPEAKER_00]: Which doesn't mean, so like, the fact that he cannot, his mind, he cannot choose those actions.

239
00:21:35,591 --> 00:21:41,037
[SPEAKER_00]: It means exactly the opposite of it meaning like his choices are not free anymore or something.

240
00:21:41,197 --> 00:21:42,679
[SPEAKER_00]: It's completely the opposite.

241
00:21:42,699 --> 00:21:50,688
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like his choice, when you said that it's like completely severed from those pressures, it makes, that's what makes it utterly free.

242
00:21:50,968 --> 00:21:51,228
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

243
00:21:51,268 --> 00:21:56,314
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the, you know, there is no reason, like it's impossible for somebody to

244
00:22:01,342 --> 00:22:17,231
[SPEAKER_02]: But if there is no original bother from which you would be seeking the relief, there is no association for acting, for doing anything towards it.

245
00:22:18,492 --> 00:22:23,435
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if the pressure, doesn't matter the intensity, cannot bother you,

246
00:22:24,890 --> 00:22:26,231
[SPEAKER_02]: Why would you be breaking the presets?

247
00:22:26,491 --> 00:22:28,632
[SPEAKER_02]: Why would you be giving in to anything on account of it?

248
00:22:30,212 --> 00:22:32,513
[SPEAKER_02]: You only do so because it still bothers you.

249
00:22:49,841 --> 00:22:50,621
[SPEAKER_02]: And it will bother you.

250
00:22:50,661 --> 00:22:52,382
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not like that's gonna stop overnight.

251
00:22:53,743 --> 00:23:01,287
[SPEAKER_02]: But the reason why I'm saying this is so that you know then, okay, it's not that pressure automatically results in action.

252
00:23:02,668 --> 00:23:11,333
[SPEAKER_02]: It's more like I have a critical point after which I can't withstand the pressure and then I choose to go this way or that way.

253
00:23:11,833 --> 00:23:13,914
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means that point needs to be upgraded.

254
00:23:15,195 --> 00:23:16,696
[SPEAKER_02]: That point needs to be developed further.

255
00:23:19,042 --> 00:23:35,387
[SPEAKER_02]: Whereby, as the Sutta says, even if highway robbers catch you and chop your limbs off and abuse you, not just you wouldn't do anything verbally or speech out of that pressure, even mentally, the Sutta says, arahant will not have thoughts of hatred towards them.

256
00:23:35,707 --> 00:23:37,728
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that was already a coping mechanism.

257
00:23:38,148 --> 00:23:38,888
[SPEAKER_02]: Hateful thoughts.

258
00:23:41,249 --> 00:23:42,469
[SPEAKER_02]: But there is nothing to cope.

259
00:23:43,169 --> 00:23:45,710
[SPEAKER_02]: Why would you be coping when nothing is bothering you to begin with?

260
00:24:19,446 --> 00:24:37,497
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in practical way, less and less acting out of pressure is better direction than acting out of pressure in direction of constant increasing, so to speak.

261
00:24:37,757 --> 00:24:46,443
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's like removing yourself from domain of acting out of pressure by

262
00:24:47,234 --> 00:25:01,864
[SPEAKER_03]: Not giving in, instead of even those small acts of acting out of pressure, it's like still there is some burning of this fire, even little, but still...

263
00:25:02,065 --> 00:25:03,245
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, yes.

264
00:25:03,326 --> 00:25:04,947
[SPEAKER_02]: In that theoretical sense, that's correct.

265
00:25:05,067 --> 00:25:16,435
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just that what constitutes acting out of pressure is not a static thing that you can just adhere to it as a method.

266
00:25:19,530 --> 00:25:24,133
[SPEAKER_02]: What constitutes what's acting out of pressure at the time, is your intention to act at the time.

267
00:25:28,375 --> 00:25:30,577
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's what needs to be refined more and more.

268
00:25:31,077 --> 00:25:37,061
[SPEAKER_02]: So first, you obviously don't act out in the obvious, coarse, visibly unwholesome ways.

269
00:25:38,702 --> 00:25:41,423
[SPEAKER_02]: And when that basis is covered, you follow the same principle.

270
00:25:43,144 --> 00:25:48,488
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you see whether your thing that you're about to do, even if it's not against the precepts,

271
00:25:49,348 --> 00:25:51,449
[SPEAKER_02]: You see, well, where is this rooted at?

272
00:25:54,590 --> 00:25:57,311
[SPEAKER_02]: Am I doing it because of some of... one of the hindrances?

273
00:25:59,152 --> 00:26:00,292
[SPEAKER_02]: Because of this, because of that?

274
00:26:01,093 --> 00:26:02,913
[SPEAKER_02]: I want to get rid of the discomfort of it?

275
00:26:04,254 --> 00:26:07,635
[SPEAKER_02]: In which case you already recognize, aha, okay, so that would be rooted in pressure.

276
00:26:08,196 --> 00:26:11,077
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then when you say no to it, your job begins.

277
00:26:11,417 --> 00:26:16,539
[SPEAKER_02]: Because often people think, aha, okay, so I just need to keep saying no to the acting out of pressure and then sotāpatti will happen.

278
00:26:18,190 --> 00:26:18,450
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

279
00:26:18,570 --> 00:26:22,754
[SPEAKER_02]: Learning how to say no to the pressure on the right level is where your practice starts.

280
00:26:24,416 --> 00:26:31,683
[SPEAKER_02]: Because when you say no to acting out of pressure, even if it's not against the precepts, for example, well, your mind will let you know about it.

281
00:26:33,097 --> 00:26:36,118
[SPEAKER_02]: If not right away, it's going to start going in that direction.

282
00:26:37,019 --> 00:26:48,704
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's going to start throwing more rational thoughts at you, more irrational thoughts at you, irrational scenarios, trying to scare you more, trying to pressure you more, just because you said no to those little things.

283
00:26:50,905 --> 00:26:51,685
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, what do you do?

284
00:26:52,106 --> 00:26:54,006
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you then explain that mind away?

285
00:26:54,567 --> 00:26:57,188
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you focus on your nostrils, so you don't have to think about it?

286
00:26:58,026 --> 00:27:02,308
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you try to distract yourself with garden work so that you don't think about what's pressuring you?

287
00:27:02,809 --> 00:27:05,690
[SPEAKER_02]: Or do you allow it to be there because that's what it is?

288
00:27:06,030 --> 00:27:06,811
[SPEAKER_02]: That's who it is.

289
00:27:07,231 --> 00:27:10,333
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what the mind is.

290
00:27:10,473 --> 00:27:14,075
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, learn to live with that type of mind.

291
00:27:14,255 --> 00:27:15,035
[SPEAKER_02]: Let's put it that way.

292
00:27:15,876 --> 00:27:17,317
[SPEAKER_02]: Not the mind on your own terms.

293
00:27:19,858 --> 00:27:26,822
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, see, by learning to live with it, you're learning to not be moved by any intensity of any scenario of any mind state.

294
00:27:29,116 --> 00:27:35,062
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why, technically, as the Buddha was saying, this can be developed even if somebody's outside of his teaching.

295
00:27:36,323 --> 00:27:40,687
[SPEAKER_02]: Obviously, they would be very wise and astute as to where the point is.

296
00:27:41,788 --> 00:27:43,750
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's not limited this mind development.

297
00:27:43,810 --> 00:27:50,176
[SPEAKER_02]: Of course, we know it now because of the Buddha's teachings, but generally, you could see somebody, for whatever reason,

298
00:27:51,242 --> 00:28:05,834
[SPEAKER_02]: Starts valuing not acting out of pressure, starts refining the same principle, but then not covering up the mind that's the result of that imposed pressure and saying no to your desires, they would force themselves to grow and become immovable.

299
00:28:10,358 --> 00:28:14,682
[SPEAKER_02]: And sometimes that mind might be in that state for days, weeks, state of doubt,

300
00:28:16,045 --> 00:28:16,966
[SPEAKER_02]: state of discomfort.

301
00:28:17,086 --> 00:28:17,527
[SPEAKER_02]: Just mind.

302
00:28:17,887 --> 00:28:18,748
[SPEAKER_02]: Everything else is fine.

303
00:28:18,828 --> 00:28:25,296
[SPEAKER_02]: But in the back of the mind... You know like if something bothers you, and you have to wait two weeks before you can resolve it.

304
00:28:27,939 --> 00:28:29,080
[SPEAKER_02]: And something's really bothering you.

305
00:28:29,280 --> 00:28:30,241
[SPEAKER_02]: It's an important thing.

306
00:28:31,042 --> 00:28:33,365
[SPEAKER_02]: But for two weeks, you have to be patient with it.

307
00:28:35,317 --> 00:28:41,041
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what your common person would do, they would just try to make those two weeks go as quickly as possible.

308
00:28:41,081 --> 00:28:46,324
[SPEAKER_02]: Engage in sensuality, engage in distraction, don't think about it until the second week comes when you can deal with it.

309
00:28:46,864 --> 00:28:50,166
[SPEAKER_02]: But now imagine you have no access to sensuality or distractions.

310
00:28:51,007 --> 00:28:55,509
[SPEAKER_02]: You just have to stay with something that will bother you for, wow, 14 days.

311
00:28:57,691 --> 00:28:59,152
[SPEAKER_02]: And not deny that mind.

312
00:29:00,632 --> 00:29:03,054
[SPEAKER_02]: Don't spend your whole day sleeping so you don't think about it.

313
00:29:03,829 --> 00:29:12,313
[SPEAKER_02]: But go about your life with this being the weather, the mood, always there.

314
00:29:12,553 --> 00:29:18,475
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the training of separating yourself correctly, of disassociating yourself from that mind correctly.

315
00:29:19,216 --> 00:29:26,659
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, imagine if, instead of two weeks, you are ready for two years, twenty years, forever.

316
00:29:29,040 --> 00:29:29,380
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

317
00:29:29,680 --> 00:29:31,481
[SPEAKER_02]: You would be free from your own mind.

318
00:29:32,322 --> 00:29:32,942
[SPEAKER_02]: If you are ready,

319
00:29:34,157 --> 00:29:42,159
[SPEAKER_02]: To be with it forever, without any traces of resistance towards it, or trying to replace it, or deal with it, or distract yourself from it, there's nothing left for you to do.

320
00:29:45,159 --> 00:29:46,560
[SPEAKER_02]: But, yeah, start with two weeks.

321
00:29:53,901 --> 00:29:55,642
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it doesn't... you don't need to do anything, by the way.

322
00:29:56,842 --> 00:30:00,803
[SPEAKER_02]: When that state's present, it's not like, oh, now I have to do this, and... you don't have to do anything.

323
00:30:04,309 --> 00:30:07,756
[SPEAKER_02]: You just have to say no to doing things that will be taking you away from it.

324
00:30:09,379 --> 00:30:10,562
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how you stay with it.

325
00:30:37,614 --> 00:30:39,635
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever.

326
00:30:41,676 --> 00:30:43,496
[SPEAKER_02]: Distracting yourself from it, yeah.

327
00:30:46,877 --> 00:30:48,018
[SPEAKER_02]: Psychologizing it.

328
00:30:50,138 --> 00:30:53,719
[SPEAKER_02]: Focusing away, moment to moment, so you don't think about it, so it disappears.

329
00:30:59,936 --> 00:31:00,136
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

330
00:31:00,676 --> 00:31:01,116
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

331
00:31:01,537 --> 00:31:02,918
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that becomes your doing again.

332
00:31:04,059 --> 00:31:05,780
[SPEAKER_02]: So, say you think, I must keep it there.

333
00:31:06,300 --> 00:31:07,401
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you ask yourself, why?

334
00:31:07,741 --> 00:31:08,782
[SPEAKER_02]: Why do I have to keep it there?

335
00:31:08,842 --> 00:31:10,243
[SPEAKER_02]: If I don't keep it, will it go away?

336
00:31:10,343 --> 00:31:10,503
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

337
00:31:12,424 --> 00:31:13,966
[SPEAKER_02]: If I keep it, will it go faster?

338
00:31:14,746 --> 00:31:16,607
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, well, that's kind of what I'm hoping.

339
00:31:17,528 --> 00:31:21,671
[SPEAKER_02]: That if I keep it there, it will disappear and stop bothering me, because I'm resisting it.

340
00:31:24,333 --> 00:31:26,514
[SPEAKER_02]: So, not pushing forward, not standing still.

341
00:31:27,995 --> 00:31:28,676
[SPEAKER_03]: As the sutra says,

342
00:31:35,775 --> 00:31:37,296
[SPEAKER_02]: And not pulling backwards, as I say.

343
00:31:43,639 --> 00:31:46,600
[SPEAKER_03]: So, basically, disown it.

344
00:31:46,780 --> 00:31:47,681
[SPEAKER_03]: Why not acting out?

345
00:31:48,741 --> 00:31:54,904
[SPEAKER_02]: Disowning would be the result of setting it up correctly and then enduring it correctly.

346
00:31:54,984 --> 00:32:00,607
[SPEAKER_02]: But if I say, yes, disown it, it's something that you would probably start to do.

347
00:32:02,272 --> 00:32:04,253
[SPEAKER_02]: Start applying the idea of disowning it.

348
00:32:04,553 --> 00:32:05,693
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's not disowning it.

349
00:32:08,294 --> 00:32:13,595
[SPEAKER_02]: Disowning means not feed it, don't let it disappear either, i.e.

350
00:32:13,635 --> 00:32:14,936
[SPEAKER_02]: don't distract yourself from it.

351
00:32:16,516 --> 00:32:17,896
[SPEAKER_02]: That will result in disowning.

352
00:32:17,936 --> 00:32:18,256
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

353
00:32:18,817 --> 00:32:23,618
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it will become apparent that you can't own it even if you want to.

